   "FavorStory_131102_Title": "On Being Hsin",
   "FavorStory_131103_Content": "When the people of the Court of Savantae surrendered themselves to the Lexicon Axis and opened their eyes once more, they found themselves in Xuanfang Hold's mainframe, within <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> Hsin's <b>Manifold Sactum</b>.\n\nThe roar of the Fortress and Gorges battlefield still rang in their ears. But before their eyes there was only silence, only emptiness, only the endless blank of nowhere at all. Frequencies threaded through one another like currents in a deep sea. Information unfolded in shapes they had no words for. Theories that had once required lifetimes to glimpse even a fragment of now stood legible in their minds, as though, having shed their earthly bodies, the Celestials had at last found a way to see the world as the Sentinel saw it.\n\nAnd when the glow enveloping them receded, they discovered, to their astonishment, that they still wore the faces they had carried in life. Some remained frozen, breathless, unable to move. Others had already begun to study the strange new architecture of their own existence. One by one, in silence, they accepted what they had become. And then, like stars flickering to life in an empty sky, new calculations kindled across the space, each one a small light in the dark.\n\nAs Celestials, they no longer felt time pass. They no longer hungered, no longer tired. On the rare occasions when they withdrew from the Celestial Ring Formation, all that met their eyes was still the same endless, patient data.\n\nHsin, ever burdened by guilt, took the form of a fox and roamed the Sanctum's halls—guiding, jesting, distracting, never quite still. At one point, She passed by a certain chamber where several Celestials had gathered around a construct of data. They spoke in hushed tones, their voices thick with something that sounded like homesickness. \n\nShe drew closer and saw what they had made: jade-green outlines rose in mountain ranges, a ribbon of flowing light wound between them like a river, and scattered points of brilliant starlight settled among the peaks, like towns glowing in the distance.\n\nThe fox watched for a long moment, and then quietly withdrew.\n\nWhen the Celestials returned to that same chamber not long after, they found the emptiness gone. Clouds rolled across distant mountains. A stream made its way down through the stones. Trees grew thick and green, their leaves catching light like water. Pavilions waited half-hidden in bamboo groves and drifting shadow. A wind moved through the eaves, carrying the cool, green smell of grass and damp earth—all of it, remarkably, resembling the homeland they remembered.\n\nThe Sentinel stood at the Inner Threshold, Her smile bright as a blade, Her hands spread with an air of courtly grace, though Her fox ears twitched with barely concealed pride. She looked at the stunned assembly before Her and spoke cheerfully.\n\n\"What, just because you've become <b>Celestials</b>, does that mean the mortal world is somehow lost to you?\"\n\"...Don't just stand there gawking. Tell me, is there anything you dislike? Oh, and while you're at it, I've been trying to name this place. Any ideas? Nothing I come up with quite fits...\"\n\nLater, this landscape received its name—<b>Mengxuan Vale</b>. Hsin bound it to Xuanfang Hold's workings, thread by thread, node by node. When a node failed, leaves would wilt on the branches. When the modular parts' operations slowed, the stream's current would likewise grow sluggish. One day, a group of Celestials passed the stream and noticed a tree whose leaves had drooped on one side. They stopped at once.\n\n\"Sector <ano=Yang Earth and Dragon>Wuchen</ano> has a few corrupted registers.\"\n\nThe Celestials vanished from where they stood, moments later returning from within the modular parts to find green already returning to the branches.\n\nHsin, watching with amusement nearby, propped her chin in her hand and said, \"Tsk tsk. Mirroring the myriad mechanisms of Xuanfang across mountains and streams, are we? Reading disorder in the droop of a single leaf… Heh. In your old lives, you'd have called that 'dashboard maintenance,' wouldn't you?\"\n\nThe Celestials exchanged glances, then shook their heads with quiet laughter, long since used to their Sentinel's jests.\n\nAnd so the years flowed on here, in a different form. Outside the Simulacrum Nexus, Xuanfang continued turning. Inside the Vale, the mortal world was never truly far away.",
   "FavorStory_131103_Title": "The Vale Not Far",
-  "FavorStory_131104_Content": "A single petal spun loose from the branch and drifted down, landing right upon the little fox's nose.\n\nShe held still for a breath or two, then sneezed, scattering the petal to the ground. The little fox shook her head and looked up at the blossoming boughs reaching out from behind the wall.\n\nYears had passed since the <te href=750053>Fortress and Gorges War</te>, and this tree, transplanted here to Xuanfang Hold, had finally bloomed again.\n \nHsin lingered beneath it for a long while before continuing down the lane. The people who had once resettled here from <te href=750052>Yuan Fortress</te> had slowly made homes for themselves throughout the city. Old signboards hung once more beneath the eaves, their painted letters faded but legible. Dust-worn tools had been wiped clean and laid out upon tables before the new houses. Shopfronts had risen one by one along the street, their hearth-smoke curling lazily into the sky, and from somewhere down the long avenue came the intermittent clang and hum of the <te href=850085>Ministry of Development</te> repairing Autopuppes. Ever since that cold yet tender-hearted Sword Transcendent had agreed to hold, in name only, the post of Xuan Paragon, the <te href=270001>Land of Xuanfang</te> had grown only more settled. People had begun to speak of next year's trade, to discuss what their village houses still lacked, to make plans in anticipation for days further off still, like next year's Waking Moon Festival.\n\nNot far off, children chased a <te href=750051>Rafter Kite</te> across the courtyard, their laughter fading into the depths of the blossoming trees. The little fox listened beneath the eaves for a while, the tip of her tail swaying quietly.\n\nThe Xuanfang Hold first shown within the <te href=800053>Civilization Capsule</te> had never looked like the city before her now. Its original design was solely to contain <te href=850081>Tacet Discords</te>, a fortress of mechanical order, its blueprints drawn in straight lines and cold logic. There had been no room in those plans for lamplight, ot the smoke of burning hearths, no corner set aside for the small, fragile warmth of human life.\n\nYet Hsin had always known that this City of Mechanisms, which defied the laws of civilization's evolution, which drew against a future not yet earned, should never have been allowed to remain in this world. Nor should the technology within it, so far beyond its age, ever be permitted to spread. Knowledge cannot be commanded unless it is truly understood. And if it spread, if it fell into the wrong hands, if it was misused—the very decisions meant to shelter civilization could instead become the seeds of endless disaster. That was why the <te href=850083>Sentinel</te>'s restrictions existed at all. \n\nBut the Xuanfang Hold before her now had, ever since the Court of Savantae took part in its building, long since woven itself into the flesh and blood of this age, nourished in turn by the soldiers and civilians who now lived within its walls. Its roots had grown deep into the earth. It was no longer merely a machine. It was a home.\n\nEven if it had been born of technology that did not belong to this age, this city was, in the end, still theirs. And after that choice She had made, the Civilization Capsule, too, had long since become inseparable from Xuanfang Hold.\n\nAnd so, for the first time, Hsin attempted to cross beyond the boundaries of authority set for the Sentinel. She began to unravel the structure thread by thread, searching for some space that might hold new rule within it. All the while keeping Xuanfang Hold running, repairing the damaged Civilization Capsule, within which still lingered a possibility She had never once let go of.\n\nJust as the first cycle of overwrite calculations began, a pair of eyes quietly opened deep within the Sanctum.\n\n<i>Sentinel authority deviation detected.</i>\n<i>Initiating correction protocol.</i>\n<i>Standard Sentinel model reconstruction commencing.</i>\n\n Hsin saw that face, no different at all from the Sentinel's own, and knew then that this long contest of wills had only just begun.",
