lueprints drawn in straight lines and cold logic. There had been no room in those plans for lamplight, ot the smoke of burning hearths, no corner set aside for the small, fragile warmth of human life.\n\nYet Hsin had always known that this City of Mechanisms, which defied the laws of civilization's evolution, which drew against a future not yet earned, should never have been allowed to remain in this world. Nor should the technology within it, so far beyond its age, ever be permitted to spread. Knowledge cannot be commanded unless it is truly understood. And if it spread, if it fell into the wrong hands, if it was misused—the very decisions meant to shelter civilization could instead become the seeds of endless disaster. That was why the <te href=850083>Sentinel</te>'s restrictions existed at all. \n\nBut the Xuanfang Hold before her now had, ever since the Court of Savantae took part in its building, long since woven itself into the flesh and blood of this age, nourished in turn by the soldiers and civilians who now lived within its walls. Its roots had grown deep into the earth. It was no longer merely a machine. It was a home.\n\nEven if it had been born of technology that did not belong to this age, this city was, in the end, still theirs. And after that choice She had made, the Civilization Capsule, too, had long since become inseparable from Xuanfang Hold.\n\nAnd so, for the first time, Hsin attempted to cross beyond the boundaries of authority set for the Sentinel. She began to unravel the structure thread by thread, searching for some space that might hold new rule within it. All the while keeping Xuanfang Hold running, repairing the damaged Civilization Capsule, within which still lingered a possibility She had never once let go of.\n\nJust as the first cycle of overwrite calculations began, a pair of eyes quietly opened deep within the Sanctum.\n\n<i>Sentinel authority deviation detected.</i>\n<i>Initiating correction protocol.</i>\n<i>Standard Sentinel model reconstruction commencing.</i>\n\nHsin saw that face, no different at all from the Sentinel's own, and knew then that this long contest of wills had only just begun.",
   "FavorStory_131104_Title": "Where Spring Stayed",
   "FavorStory_131105_Content": "Hsin held a Moon Lantern and stood alone on a secluded lakeshore. Not long ago, She had invited the Arbiter here to release lanterns, and {Male=he;Female=she} had asked what She would write upon Hers.\n\nThe <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> exists only to receive the wishes of others and offer guidance. Since when has there ever been a \"deity\" who made a wish for themselves? Back then, Hsin had only smiled and replied: I am entrusted with the wishes of all. How could I release a lantern of my own?\n\nSpoken with such easy grace.\n\nYet now, with no one around, She had quietly returned to this very spot, brush lifted and lingering over the lantern's face for a long while. Her wish was not hard to write, for it was also Her charge: May civilization flourish, may the lanterns burn eternal, may the people of this land find their belonging, and peace attend every year.\n\nAnd yet there seemed to be something else. Something simple, yet somehow more complicated still.\n\nThe tip of the brush hesitated over the paper when Her fox ears gave a faint twitch. She had caught the sound of children's startled cries in the distance.\n\nA Moon Lantern had drifted among the rocks along the shore and lodged there. A child lay stretched along the bank, gripping a branch, trying again and again to nudge it free, while a companion tugging at his sleeve grew tearful with worry. Hsin let out a soft sigh, set Her own lantern aside, and flicked a finger idly toward the distance. As though moved by some unseen hand, the trapped lantern gave a gentle spin, slipped free of the rocks, and rejoined the river of lights.\n\nThe child's sobs turned to laughter, and the Sentinel's lips curved to match. Lantern after lantern drifted past Her, each bearing wishes for safety, reunion, honor, or love, and countless small, trivial hopes besides. Hsin watched them flow with the current as though watching the countless lives of this land, long or brief, each carrying its own flickering light, journeying onward in cheerful procession. She walked slowly back through the Xuanfang Gardens on the night wind. On the water stage, \"The Fox and the Moon\" had reached its liveliest scene. The fragrance of wine and the sweetness of pastries wove through the air, rushing toward Her together with waves of laughter rising and falling all around.\n\nBefore She realized it, Her gaze had drifted past the bustling crowd to find Her Arbiter standing amid the brightest lanterns, surrounded by the throng. Moonlight and the glow of lanterns fell across that ever-composed face, gilding those golden eyes, often veiled in quiet distance, with an unmistakable tenderness.\n\nLook... the figure who, in Hsin the Moon Fox's memory, had always walked alone now stood among the crowd, lit by the lights of this mortal world. Watching that figure, She realized the distant notion of the \"Arbiter\" in Her heart had, without Her noticing, taken on an unmistakably clear shape. And on this night when the lanterns burned bright as day, the \"She\" that belonged to the Sentinel seemed, for a brief while, to have receded somewhat.\n\n...{PlayerName}.\n\n\"She\" repeated the name softly in her heart, the name the Arbiter had chosen for {Male=himself;Female=herself}, one that connected past and present as if by chance, and yet as if by fate. A name that reached past old duties and an unspeakable history, to settle simply on the person standing before her now.\n\n—Just as She, upon first arriving in Xuanfang, had once carried a wish not yet fully understood, and named herself \"Hsin.\"\n\nTo the Sentinel, the \"Arbiter\" was simply Its connection to {Male=him;Female=her}. Yet in this moment, She found herself wanting to speak {Male=his;Female=her} name instead.\n\n...Was it because doing so felt like She, too, had truly stepped into this mortal world of lights She so cherished? The thought made Her lower her eyes and smile despite Herself. When She looked up again, that unspoken feeling had been quietly tucked away in her heart, carried off along with the Moon Lantern she had never managed to release.\n\nJust then, as if sensing something, that person looked toward her across the crowd and drifting lantern light.\n\n...Perhaps it was not so complicated after all. In this moment, Hsin simply wanted to go to that person's side.\n\nShe met that smiling gaze with a crinkle of Her own eyes, and walked through the lantern-lit crowd toward {Male=him;Female=her}, steps light and quick, fox tail swaying softly with each stride.\n\n\"Arbiter—!\"",
